{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "@id": "https://kubailabs.com/mentoring-without-formal-authority-the-invisible-mandate/#faq",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I mentor someone who doesn't want to be mentored?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "You can't force it, and you shouldn't try. Start by asking what they want to get better at, and if they have no interest, offer a low-commitment alternative: \"If you ever want a second pair of eyes on a design, I'm around.\" Then step back. Forcing mentoring creates resentment and wastes both people's time. If the organisation expects you to mentor this person, talk to their manager about the mismatch — but do not turn it into a performance issue."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What if my mentee's manager sees me as a threat?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "This is common in organisations with unclear roles. The fix is transparency and deference. Tell the manager what you are doing, ask if they have any concerns, and make it clear you are not trying to replace them. If the manager remains threatened, reduce your involvement and focus on artefacts (templates, docs) rather than direct advice. You can also ask a skip-level manager to clarify roles, but that is a last resort."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How do I measure whether mentoring is working?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Don't measure it with metrics like \"number of sessions\" or \"PRs reviewed.\" Measure it by changes in the mentee's behaviour: do they bring you problems earlier? Do they ask better questions? Do they apply a pattern you discussed in a different context? These are leading indicators. Lagging indicators (promotions, performance ratings) are influenced by many factors and are not a fair test of mentoring."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Can I mentor someone more senior than me?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, but the framing changes. You are not teaching them your job; you are offering a different perspective. Be explicit about what you can offer: \"I can help you think through the API design because I've worked with similar systems, but you know the domain better than I do.\" Respect their expertise and focus on the specific gap you can fill."
      }
    }
  ]
}
